    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Thinking back to your childhood or school years, did you ever struggle with staying focused, being organized, or sitting still?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, boy, did I ever. Even as a little kid in grade school, I was the one who just could not stay in his seat. I was always blurting out the answers before the teacher even finished asking, and I got so many notes sent home to my parents for talking too much. \n\nI remember my third-grade teacher, Mrs. Higgins, she used to write those notes on this thick yellow paper. They always smelled like the mimeograph machine. You remember those? With the purple ink? Man, I loved that smell. Kids today have no idea. Everything is iPads and smartboards now. My grandson, he's nine, and he doesn't even know how to read cursive! Can you believe that? They just stopped teaching it in his district. I asked him to read a birthday card my sister sent him, and he just stared at it like it was Greek. I mean, how are these kids going to sign a check when they grow up? Or read historical documents? The Declaration of Independence is in cursive, for crying out loud. It just seems like a real shame to let a skill like that die out just because everyone wants to type on a screen... \n\nWait, what was the question again? I know we were talking about school.",
